Core Concepts to Master (regardless of question)
Whatever question you get, FAANG and product companies expect strong knowledge of these building blocks. Scalability fundamentals like horizontal vs vertical scaling, load balancers, and stateless services come up everywhere. Database design covers SQL vs NoSQL trade-offs, sharding, replication, indexing, and CAP theorem. Caching strategies include cache-aside, write-through, write-back, eviction policies (LRU/LFU), and where to place caches. Messaging involves queues (Kafka, RabbitMQ, SQS), pub-sub patterns, and event-driven architecture. Consistency models like strong vs eventual consistency and consensus algorithms (Raft, Paxos) appear in senior interviews. Finally, reliability concepts like rate limiting, circuit breakers, retries with exponential backoff, and idempotency are tested heavily at PayPal, JPMorgan, and Agoda.
LLD Concepts to Master
For LLD specifically, master the SOLID principles (especially Single Responsibility and Open/Closed), the most common design patterns (Singleton, Factory, Strategy, Observer, Decorator, Builder, Adapter, State), and how to model real-world entities into classes with clear responsibilities. Always think about extensibility: "If I had to add a new feature later, would I need to modify existing code or just add new classes?"
How to Approach Any System Design Question
Use this framework every time: clarify requirements (functional + non-functional, scale, QPS), define APIs, estimate capacity (storage, bandwidth, QPS), sketch high-level architecture (load balancer → app servers → cache → DB), deep-dive into one or two components the interviewer cares about, then discuss trade-offs and bottlenecks. Always think out loud and ask clarifying questions — interviewers care about the process, not just the final answer.

My Recommended Study Order
Start with URL Shortener since it teaches almost every core concept (hashing, DB design, caching, scaling reads/writes). Move to Rate Limiter and LRU Cache which appear in nearly every interview. Then tackle Twitter/News Feed and WhatsApp/Chat since they teach feed generation, fan-out, and real-time communication. For LLD, start with Parking Lot (the canonical LLD question) followed by Elevator, Library Management, and Splitwise. Practice each by drawing the architecture and writing class diagrams on paper or a whiteboard tool before looking at solutions.
